Database schema migrations
==========================

Linux deployment
----------------
Ship this entire folder, including versions and script.py.mako, with every
release. deploy_dashboard.sh calls dashboard_db.py, which backs up SQLite,
applies migrations, and then seeds data. Failure prevents application startup.
The backup uses SQLite's backup API so committed WAL data is included.

An empty database receives the initial schema and all subsequent revisions.
An unversioned existing database is compared against a temporary database
created from the initial revision. Only a matching schema is stamped at the
initial revision; subsequent revisions are then applied normally. Mismatches
and unknown old revision IDs stop deployment without automatically stamping.
Missing migration files also stop deployment; migrations are not generated
on the production server.

First-time initial schema generation (only if no history exists)
---------------------------------------------------------------
On a development checkout, use models matching the existing production
schema and a NEW EMPTY scratch database. Do not point at production:

    export FLASK_APP=manage.py
    export DATABASE_URL=sqlite:////absolute/path/to/new_baseline_scratch.db
    python -m flask db init       # only when migrations/ does not exist
    python -m flask db migrate -m "initial schema"
    python -m flask db upgrade

Then update the models to the new release and generate the delta against
that scratch database:

    python -m flask db migrate -m "add PAY_VM column"
    # Review the file, including defaults/backfills for existing rows.
    python -m flask db upgrade
    unset DATABASE_URL

Commit and ship both revisions. Our initial schema file already exists;
do not generate a second initial revision. If the production schema is older
than this initial schema, prepare a reviewed reconciliation before deploying.

This folder starts with one complete initial schema: 0001_initial.
It represents the current models and creates tables, constraints and indexes.
It does not insert application seed data.

New EMPTY database (PowerShell, from project root)
------------------------------------------------
Set DATABASE_URL to the intended empty database, then run:

    $env:FLASK_APP = "manage.py"
    python -m flask db upgrade

Use manage.py for migration commands: it skips startup create_all, seeding
and runtime initialization. Do not run db init again.

Existing database
-----------------
Do not run the initial upgrade against existing application tables.
Back up the database, compare its schema with the models, and reconcile any
missing tables, columns or constraints before marking the initial revision
as applied. Stamping records a revision; it does not update the schema.

The local dashboard.db still records the retired revision d9a4b7c8e1f2.
Its data and revision record were deliberately not changed during cleanup.
It needs schema reconciliation and replacement of the retired revision
record before it can use this new history. Do not merely stamp it to hide
missing columns. A database already matching the complete initial schema
can be adopted by stamping 0001_initial (purging a retired revision record
if necessary), after backup and schema verification.

Future model changes
--------------------
Against a database already at the current migration head:

    $env:FLASK_APP = "manage.py"
    python -m flask db migrate -m "describe schema change"

Review the generated revision, then:

    python -m flask db upgrade

Commit migration files with the models. Never edit applied revisions.
Review renames manually; autogeneration can treat them as drop/add changes.
New required columns need a backfill or server default for existing rows.
Downgrading the initial revision to base deletes all application tables.
